<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    PowerFramework.Security.Tests
    Unit and service level tests for the Security service of the PowerFramework .NET 10
    decomposition.

    Security is the sole JWT issuer in the decomposed system and owns the keyed cryptographic
    surface ported from the legacy Appeon PowerBuilder framework. The behaviour under test is
    specified by the read only legacy tree, which is the behavioural oracle. Legacy objects are
    cited here BY LOCATOR ONLY and are never consumed as a build input by this project:

      ws_objects/pfw.crypto.pbl.src/n_crypto.sru:L9 to L73
        The keyed cryptographic surface, roughly 62 overloads: unkeyed hash and keyed HMAC over
        the string and blob cross product, symmetric encrypt and decrypt, RSA encrypt, decrypt,
        sign and verify, RSA key generation, random blob, random string and GUID generation, and
        the encoding conversions.

      ws_objects/pfw.shared.pbl.src/enums.sru:L924 to L967
        The CRYPTO_ algorithm, mode, padding and flag catalogue, whose identifier spellings are
        preserved verbatim because they appear in serialized payloads, log records and
        characterization recordings. It carries the legacy weak defaults the port replicates as
        annotated defaults, notably ECB as the default symmetric mode and PKCS1 as the default
        RSA padding.

    PROJECT SHAPE. Every line below is load bearing, not stylistic:

      * Microsoft.NET.Sdk, deliberately NOT the web SDK variant. The web SDK belongs to the
        application project; this project consumes that application's host rather than being one.

      * OutputType Exe is MANDATORY, and is declared explicitly rather than left to inheritance.
        It is the property xunit.v3 itself validates: the package's build targets raise a hard
        error when the effective value is anything other than Exe, because a v3 test assembly runs
        as its own process instead of being loaded as a library the way a v2 one was. Measured on
        this toolchain, SDK 10.0.302 with the pinned test SDK, that test SDK also forces the value
        from its own targets, so today the explicit declaration is belt as well as braces. It stays
        regardless, because this project must satisfy the test framework's contract on its own
        terms rather than depend on a transitive package continuing to set it: without the
        declaration the project is one package upgrade or one asset restriction away from building
        as a library that discovers no test at all, which would disable the coverage gate silently.

      * Hand authored, never templated. In SDK 10.0.302 there is no `dotnet new xunit3` template,
        and `dotnet new xunit` scaffolds the v2 line with an older test SDK and older coverage
        packages. Any templated output must be discarded rather than patched.

      * TargetFramework, LangVersion, Nullable, ImplicitUsings, TreatWarningsAsErrors and
        ManagePackageVersionsCentrally are inherited from the repository root
        Directory.Build.props by MSBuild directory walk up. They are deliberately NOT restated
        here: a restatement is duplication that can silently drift away from the repository wide
        quality gate. Warnings are errors in this project exactly as they are everywhere else.

      * Every PackageReference below is versionless. Central Package Management is enabled
        repository wide, so the single source of truth for package versions is the repository root
        Directory.Packages.props. That indirection is the mechanism which forces the mandatory
        security pins onto every service from one place, so no service can drift onto a vulnerable
        transitive package. No version attribute may ever be added to a reference in this file; a
        NuGet audit finding is fixed centrally, never pinned locally.

    INDEPENDENT BUILD AND TEST. This service builds and tests from a clean checkout with no other
    service, no shared solution and no orchestration:

        cd services/security-service
        dotnet restore
        dotnet build -c Release
        dotnet test with the XPlat Code Coverage collector

    Those bare commands resolve because services/security-service/security-service.slnx is the
    only solution file in that directory and it enumerates this project at exactly
    PowerFramework.Security.Tests/PowerFramework.Security.Tests.csproj. A .slnf solution filter
    must never be authored alongside it: the migration plan records the legacy solution filter
    format as incompatible with the .slnx format, breaking restore, build and test alike, so the
    per service .slnx is the only supported entry point for this service.

    Note for anyone reading a coverage report: a bare `dotnet test` builds Debug even immediately
    after `dotnet build -c Release`. The release configuration form passes -c Release to the test
    command as well. That is documented toolchain behaviour recorded in docs/BUILD.md and must not
    be worked around from inside this project file.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- Required by xunit.v3, which runs the test assembly as its own process. See the header. -->
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <!-- A test assembly is never shipped as a NuGet package. -->
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Implicit using for the test framework namespace">
    <!--
      The additive companion to the repository wide ImplicitUsings setting, and the reason this
      item is here rather than left out: the xunit.v3 package ships no implicit using of its own,
      while the established test project convention, which the SDK's own test template follows, is
      that the PROJECT declares the test framework namespace globally and the test source files
      therefore do not repeat it. Without this item every sibling test class would have to declare
      the namespace itself or fail to compile with CS0246 on Fact, Theory and IClassFixture. This
      item is not a package reference and carries no version.
    -->
    <Using Include="Xunit" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Project coupling: the sibling Security application project, and nothing else">
    <!--
      Exactly one ProjectReference, by design. Types from the shared kernel and from the published
      contracts project arrive TRANSITIVELY through this reference. Reaching them with a direct
      reference, or referencing any other service or any other service's test project, would
      create precisely the cross service coupling the architecture forbids: the published
      contracts are the only sanctioned coupling between services and no shared behaviour crosses
      a service boundary.

      The referenced project also owns the test visibility declaration and the appsettings content
      behaviour that the sibling test classes rely on, so neither is duplicated here.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\PowerFramework.Security\PowerFramework.Security.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Test stack: versionless, pinned centrally in Directory.Packages.props">
    <!-- Test framework and assertion library. -->
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3" />

    <!-- Adapter so the standard test command discovers xunit.v3 tests. -->
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio"
                      PrivateAssets="all"
                      IncludeAssets="runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive" />

    <!-- Test host. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" />

    <!--
      Emits coverage.cobertura.xml, which is the exact artifact the 80 percent line coverage per
      service gate in .github/workflows/ci.yml is measured from. Without this reference the gate
      cannot be evaluated at all and CI fails for a structural reason rather than a quality one,
      so the reference is a hard requirement and not a convenience.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="coverlet.collector"
                      PrivateAssets="all"
                      IncludeAssets="runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive" />

    <!--
      In process host for the service level tests. Required HERE even though the shared library
      test projects deliberately omit it, because this service exposes an HTTP surface, namely
      token issuance, the JWKS and OpenID discovery metadata, the crypto endpoints, the anonymous
      health endpoint and the authenticated ping endpoint, which SecurityAppFactory.cs boots in
      process. Hand rolling a host instead is not an option: the factory must share the
      application's own service registrations so that the deterministic clock and entropy doubles
      substitute for the real ones, which is what makes the random, GUID and expiry dependent
      behaviour reproducible for characterization. This package carries a framework reference to
      Microsoft.AspNetCore.App, so no additional package or framework reference is needed.

      No further package belongs in this project. No mocking library, no fluent assertion library
      and no third party cryptographic library: the base class library covers every operation of
      the legacy cryptographic surface, and the clock and entropy doubles are hand authored in
      SecurityAppFactory.cs rather than taken from a package.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing" />
  </ItemGroup>


  <!--
    REPOSITORY ROOT, EMBEDDED SO THIS PROJECT'S ON DISK LOCATORS DO NOT DEPEND ON WHERE ITS OUTPUT WENT
    This project reads real files from the source tree, and it finds them by walking up from
    AppContext.BaseDirectory for a marker. That walk holds only while the output sits inside the
    checkout, so a test run whose artifacts path option names a directory outside the tree - a supported
    and entirely valid way to run the suite - left every one of those locators with no ancestor
    carrying the marker, and they threw for a reason unrelated to the code under test.

    The value comes from the PowerFrameworkRepositoryRoot property in the repository root
    Directory.Build.props, which resolves it from that file's own location. It is turned into assembly
    metadata HERE, in each test project, rather than there: a service assembly ships inside a container
    image and must carry no build machine path, and the root property sheet keeps its documented rule of
    introducing no project level item. The locators still verify their marker and still keep the walk as
    the fallback, so an absent or stale value degrades to the previous behaviour rather than misreading
    a different tree.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <AssemblyMetadata Include="PowerFrameworkRepositoryRoot" Value="$(PowerFrameworkRepositoryRoot)" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
